  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder \nof October with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nA weak front to our south will retreat northward as a warm front\nWednesday morning. A large area of high pressure will build to \nthe north Thursday through Saturday. As a result, expect passing\nhigh clouds and a series of wind shifts with each of the \nfrontal passages. Winds will change to the south and southwest \nlate Wednesday through Friday. This will yield seasonable to \nabove normal temperatures for the remainder of the workweek. \nHighs will generally range from the mid 60s and low 70s though \nWednesday with low to mid 70s Thursday and mid to upper 70s \nFriday. This is in part to the position of the high to the \nnorth leading to increased southeast to southwest winds across \nthe region. Overnight lows will remain mild as well in the mid \n40s and low 50s.\n\nThere is the potential for some low clouds, fog, or even a\nlittle drizzle during the late night into morning hours each of\nthe next few days as onshore flow develops.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...On-off rain showers are possible beginning Sunday\ninto early next week.\n\nSurface high pressure lingers on Friday before tracking north of the \nregion on Saturday, with generally sunny & warm conditions \npersisting through then. Highs on Friday could reach into the mid-to-\nupper 70s across the metros as this persistent high pressure has \nallowed for temperatures to gradually build throughout the week.\n\nMeanwhile, a broad area of low pressure will form over the Midwest \nand then track east late in the weekend, bringing increasing cloud \ncover to the region throughout the day Saturday. A stemming cold \nfront will cause temperatures to drop Saturday as this \nsystem tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, with potential rain showers \nbeginning on Sunday. Uncertainty remains high on total spread and \nrainfall accumulation as the system looks somewhat disorganized in \nmodel guidance. Regardless, this will be the first chance for rain \nin well over a week across the region. While temperatures drop back \ninto the upper 60s Saturday & Sunday, this remains somewhat above \naverage for the start of November. \n\nAs this system tracks offshore by Tuesday, a second, potentially \nmore potent upper-level low could form over the Plains and make its \nway to the region by the middle of next week. Uncertainty remains \nhigh regarding the track of this system, but this could bring \nadditional rainfall to the region as we get into November.",
